@* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   SectionErrorBoundary — one card fails, the page stays.

   AppErrorBoundary catches anything that escapes a page and replaces the whole
   screen with "Something went wrong". That is right for a page that cannot
   render at all and wrong for a chart that threw: a bad heatmap payload used to
   take the answer, the schedule and every other card down with it.

   Wrap each independently-failing region in one of these. A throw during its
   render or lifecycle is contained to a small inline notice with a Retry, which
   calls Recover() and re-renders the children from scratch — usually with the
   fresh data the page's own refresh has brought in by then.

   Knows nothing about routes: Label is the only thing it says about what failed.
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@

<ErrorBoundary @ref="_boundary">
    <ChildContent>
        @ChildContent
    </ChildContent>
    <ErrorContent>
        <div class="pt-section-error" role="alert">
            <PtIcon Name="error" Size="sm" Class="pt-section-error-icon" />
            <span class="pt-section-error-text">Couldn't show @Label.</span>
            <button type="button" class="pt-section-error-retry pt-tap" @onclick="Recover">Retry</button>
        </div>
    </ErrorContent>
</ErrorBoundary>

@code {
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the region is, in words that finish "Couldn't show …".</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "this section";

    private ErrorBoundary? _boundary;

    private void Recover() => _boundary?.Recover();
}
